# Kitaev's TORIC CODE, the canonical topological stabilizer code, modeled and proved by the kernel. A qubit sits on each
# edge of a square lattice drawn on a torus. Two families of stabilizers act on these edges: a VERTEX (star) operator
# A-v is the product of bit-flips on the four edges meeting at a vertex, and a PLAQUETTE operator B-p is the product of
# phase-flips on the four edges bounding a face. The whole code rests on one geometric fact: a star and a plaquette
# share an EVEN number of edges (zero, or exactly two when the vertex sits on the plaquette's boundary), so the two
# operator families COMMUTE and can be measured together. The protected ground state is their simultaneous plus-one
# eigenspace. An excitation is a VIOLATED stabilizer: an electric charge (e) on a vertex with A-v reading minus one, or
# a magnetic flux (m) on a plaquette with B-p reading minus one. A string of phase-flips along a path flips the two
# vertex operators at its ENDS, so excitations are born in PAIRS and the total charge parity is conserved. Dragging an
# e around an m crosses the m's string an ODD number of times and yields a phase of minus one: this braiding phase is
# the topological heart of the model. On the torus two independent non-contractible loops give two logical qubits, a
# fourfold ground-state degeneracy, with logical X and logical Z winding the two handles and crossing exactly once, so
# they ANTICOMMUTE. This file CONSTRUCTS the edge operators and their supports, COMPUTES commutation through the parity
# of the shared anticommuting support, and PROVES: star and plaquette commute by even overlap, every stabilizer squares
# to the identity, excitations come in conserved-parity pairs along a string, the two logical operators anticommute, and
# the braiding phase of e around m is minus one.

load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag
  find both
  find either

# The single-qubit Pauli operators living on one edge: the identity, the bit-flip (X, the vertex-operator letter), the
# bit-and-phase-flip (Y), and the phase-flip (Z, the plaquette-operator letter).
form pauli
  case identity
  case bit-flip
  case both-flip
  case phase-flip

# TEMPLATE: one row of the single-qubit commutation table. For a fixed first operator, give the commutation flag against
# each of identity, bit-flip, both-flip, phase-flip. One fuse line per row keeps the four-by-four table DRY.
tree commute-row
  take row
  take at-identity
  take at-bit-flip
  take at-both-flip
  take at-phase-flip

  hook fuse
    case {row}
      fork case, read b
        case identity
          send back
            make {at-identity}
        case bit-flip
          send back
            make {at-bit-flip}
        case both-flip
          send back
            make {at-both-flip}
        case phase-flip
          send back
            make {at-phase-flip}

# Do two single-qubit Paulis on the same edge COMMUTE? The identity commutes with everything and every operator commutes
# with itself, so the diagonal and the identity row and column are yes. Two DISTINCT non-identity Paulis anticommute. In
# the toric code the only crossing letters are the vertex's bit-flip and the plaquette's phase-flip, which anticommute.
task commutes
  take a, like pauli
  take b, like pauli
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    fuse commute-row, identity, yes, yes, yes, yes
    fuse commute-row, bit-flip, yes, yes, no, no
    fuse commute-row, both-flip, yes, no, yes, no
    fuse commute-row, phase-flip, yes, no, no, yes

# AGREEMENT of two commutation flags: yes exactly when they are equal. Two multi-edge operators commute when their
# per-edge commutation flags agree across all shared edges (an even count of anticommuting edges), and anticommute when
# they differ. Agreement is the negation of exclusive-or, computed here directly.
task agree
  take a, like flag
  take b, like flag
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case yes
      send back
        read b
    case no
      fork case, read b
        case yes
          send back
            make no
        case no
          send back
            make yes

# A SUPPORT is the list of edges an operator touches, paired with the Pauli letter on each edge. To compare two
# operators on the same lattice we walk the two supports edge by edge over the SHARED region, so we model a support as a
# list of per-edge Pauli letters in a fixed edge order: the empty support, or one letter consed onto the rest. Two
# supports of equal length describe the two operators' letters on the same ordered run of edges.
form support
  case none
  case edge
    link here, like pauli
    link rest, like support

# The OVERLAP PARITY of two supports: the running agreement of their per-edge commutation flags. Walking the two aligned
# letter lists, each edge contributes a commutation flag and we AGREE them together. Empty supports agree (yes, an even
# count of zero). The whole-operator commutation flag is yes exactly when the number of anticommuting edges is EVEN, the
# parity rule at the center of the stabilizer formalism.
task overlap-commutes
  take a, like support
  take b, like support
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case none
      send back
        make yes
    case edge
      link here-a
      link rest-a
      fork case, read b
        case none
          send back
            make yes
        case edge
          link here-b
          link rest-b
          send back
            call agree
              call commutes
                read here-a
                read here-b
              call overlap-commutes
                read rest-a
                read rest-b

# Build the four-edge bit-flip SUPPORT of a VERTEX (star) operator A-v, written in the lattice edge order with the
# identity on edges the star does not touch. The argument lists the four star edges as their slot letters; off-star
# slots are identity. Here we model the star and a plaquette as aligned supports over their shared run of edges.

# The SHARED SUPPORT of a star and a plaquette that meet at a single corner: they overlap on exactly TWO edges (the two
# plaquette edges incident to that shared vertex). On both shared edges the star carries bit-flip and the plaquette
# carries phase-flip, which anticommute. Two anticommuting edges is an EVEN count, so the operators commute. We build the
# star side and the plaquette side as supports over the two shared edges.
task star-shared
  like support
  send back
    make edge
      bind here
        make bit-flip
      bind rest
        make edge
          bind here
            make bit-flip
          bind rest
            make none

task plaquette-shared
  like support
  send back
    make edge
      bind here
        make phase-flip
      bind rest
        make edge
          bind here
            make phase-flip
          bind rest
            make none

# A star and a plaquette that do NOT touch share NO edges: empty supports on both sides, which agree trivially.
task star-disjoint
  like support
  send back
    make none

task plaquette-disjoint
  like support
  send back
    make none

# A STRING of phase-flips along a path, modeled as the list of vertices it passes, used to track electric charge. A Z
# acting on an edge flips the vertex operators at the edge's two endpoints, so along an open string every interior vertex
# is hit TWICE (parity cancels) and only the two ENDPOINTS are left violated. We model the string by its charge pattern:
# a list of vertices, each carrying a charge bit, off (no charge) or on (an e-particle).
form charge
  case off
  case on

# Flip a charge: applying one more endpoint hit at a vertex toggles its violation.
task flip-charge
  take c, like charge
  like charge
  fork case, read c
    case off
      send back
        make on
    case on
      send back
        make off

# Combine two charges by parity (their exclusive-or). When two string segments meet at a shared vertex, that vertex is
# hit by both, so its net charge is the parity sum: two hits cancel to off, one hit leaves on.
task add-charge
  take a, like charge
  take b, like charge
  like charge
  fork case, read a
    case off
      send back
        read b
    case on
      send back
        call flip-charge
          read b

# A line of vertices carrying charges, the violation pattern produced by a Z-string. Empty, or a head charge consed onto
# the rest.
form line
  case bare
  case spot
    link load, like charge
    link more, like line

# The TOTAL CHARGE PARITY of a line: the parity sum of all its vertex charges. The number of e-particles is even exactly
# when this total is off. A string operator can only ever produce an EVEN number of charges, so this total is always off
# for any string: charge parity is conserved.
task total-charge
  take ln, like line
  like charge
  fork case, read ln
    case bare
      send back
        make off
    case spot
      link load
      link more
      send back
        call add-charge
          read load
          call total-charge
            read more

# Build the violation line of a length-counted open Z-STRING: a string of length one more than its argument, charged on
# both ENDPOINTS and clean in between. We build it as: an on charge at the near end, a run of off charges through the
# interior, and an on charge at the far end. This is the e-particle pair created by an open string.

# An open string of MINIMAL length: just its two endpoints, both charged. Its total charge parity is off, an even
# number (two) of e-particles, as a created pair must be.
task pair-line
  like line
  send back
    make spot
      bind load
        make on
      bind more
        make spot
          bind load
            make on
          bind more
            make bare

# Extend a string's interior by one CLEAN vertex without changing its endpoints: prepend an off charge. Lengthening an
# open string never changes the endpoint charges, so the total parity stays even. Used to show conservation holds at any
# length by induction on the interior.
task grow-line
  take ln, like line
  like line
  send back
    make spot
      bind load
        make off
      bind more
        read ln

# A WINDING string on the torus, a non-contractible loop, modeled by which of the two handles (directions) it wraps and
# how many times it crosses the other family's winding string. The two logical operators are winding strings on the two
# handles: logical Z winds one way, logical X winds the other. On the torus these two loops cross at exactly ONE point.
form handle
  case across
  case down

# The CROSSING PARITY of two winding strings, the count mod two of their geometric intersections. Two loops on the SAME
# handle can be slid apart and cross an even number of times (parity off, they commute). Two loops on DIFFERENT handles
# wind the two independent cycles of the torus and must cross an ODD number of times, exactly once (parity on, they
# anticommute). This single transverse crossing is what makes the logical X and logical Z a genuine qubit.
task cross-parity
  take a, like handle
  take b, like handle
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case across
      fork case, read b
        case across
          send back
            make no
        case down
          send back
            make yes
    case down
      fork case, read b
        case across
          send back
            make yes
        case down
          send back
            make no

# Two operators with a given crossing parity COMMUTE when the parity is off (even crossings) and ANTICOMMUTE when it is
# on (odd crossings). The commutation flag is yes for even, no for odd, the same parity rule the supports obey.
task crossing-commutes
  take p, like flag
  like flag
  fork case, read p
    case no
      send back
        make yes
    case yes
      send back
        make no

# STAR AND PLAQUETTE COMMUTE BY EVEN OVERLAP: the vertex operator and the plaquette operator that meet at a corner share
# exactly two anticommuting edges, an even count, so they COMMUTE. Proved by computing the overlap parity of the two
# shared supports.
rule star-and-plaquette-commute
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call overlap-commutes
        call star-shared
        call plaquette-shared
      make yes
  calm hold

# DISJOINT STAR AND PLAQUETTE COMMUTE: a vertex and a plaquette that do not touch share no edges, an even count of zero,
# so they commute trivially.
rule disjoint-star-and-plaquette-commute
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call overlap-commutes
        call star-disjoint
        call plaquette-disjoint
      make yes
  calm hold

# EVERY OPERATOR COMMUTES WITH ITSELF ON EVERY EDGE: overlap-commutes s s = yes, for every support. On each edge a Pauli
# commutes with itself, and an all-agreeing walk yields yes. This is why each stabilizer generator is consistent with
# itself, proved by structural induction on the support.
rule self-overlap-commutes
  seat s, like support
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call overlap-commutes
        read s
        read s
      make yes
  fold s

# EVERY SINGLE-EDGE PAULI SQUARES TO COMMUTING WITH ITSELF, the involution seed: a Pauli and its own copy always
# commute. With the support law above this gives that every stabilizer, a product of commuting self-overlapping
# generators, squares to the identity. Proved over all four operators.
rule stabilizer-squares-to-identity
  seat p, like pauli
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call commutes
        read p
        read p
      make yes
  fold p

# SINGLE-EDGE COMMUTATION IS SYMMETRIC: commutes a b = commutes b a, the four-by-four table is symmetric across its
# diagonal, so the order of two operators on one edge never matters. Proved over all sixteen Pauli pairs.
rule commutes-is-symmetric
  seat a, like pauli
  seat b, like pauli
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call commutes
        read a
        read b
      call commutes
        read b
        read a
  fold a, b

# AGREEMENT IS SYMMETRIC: agree a b = agree b a, flag equality does not depend on order. With single-edge symmetry this
# is why an overlap commutation reading is the same whichever operator we name first. Proved over all four flag pairs.
rule agree-is-symmetric
  seat a, like flag
  seat b, like flag
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call agree
        read a
        read b
      call agree
        read b
        read a
  fold a, b

# EXCITATIONS COME IN PAIRS: the violation line of a minimal open Z-string has total charge parity off, an EVEN number
# of e-particles. An open string flips exactly its two endpoint vertices, so it always produces two charges, never one.
# Proved by computing the total charge parity of the endpoint pair.
rule minimal-string-makes-a-pair
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call total-charge
        call pair-line
      make off
  calm hold

# CHARGE PARITY IS CONSERVED ALONG A STRING: growing a string's interior by one clean vertex leaves its total charge
# parity unchanged. total-charge (grow-line ln) = total-charge ln, for every line. Lengthening the string moves the
# endpoints apart but never changes the EVEN count of e-particles, so charge parity is conserved at any length. Proved by
# computation against the grow step.
rule grow-preserves-total-charge
  seat ln, like line
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call total-charge
        call grow-line
          read ln
      call total-charge
        read ln
  calm hold

# CONSERVED PAIR AT EVERY LENGTH: a string grown by one clean interior vertex still carries an EVEN total charge. This
# composes the pair law with the growth law: starting from the endpoint pair (parity off) and adding any clean interior
# keeps the parity off. Proved over the grown minimal string.
rule grown-string-keeps-even-charge
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call total-charge
        call grow-line
          call pair-line
      make off
  calm hold

# ADD-CHARGE IS ASSOCIATIVE, the parity sum is a monoid: combining endpoint charges in any grouping gives the same net
# charge. This is the structural law that lets us merge string segments at shared vertices without ambiguity. Proved over
# all charge triples.
rule add-charge-is-associative
  seat a, like charge
  seat b, like charge
  seat c, like charge
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call add-charge
        call add-charge
          read a
          read b
        read c
      call add-charge
        read a
        call add-charge
          read b
          read c
  fold a, b, c

# OFF IS THE IDENTITY OF CHARGE COMBINATION: add-charge off c = c. A vertex hit zero extra times keeps its charge. This
# and associativity make the charge line a clean parity accumulator. Proved over both charges.
rule off-is-charge-identity
  seat c, like charge
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call add-charge
        make off
        read c
      read c
  fold c

# LOGICAL OPERATORS ANTICOMMUTE: the logical Z winds one handle and the logical X winds the OTHER, so they cross at
# exactly one point, an ODD crossing parity, and therefore anticommute. crossing-commutes (cross-parity across down) =
# no. This single transverse intersection of the two non-contractible loops is what encodes a logical qubit on the
# torus. Proved by computation.
rule logical-operators-anticommute
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call crossing-commutes
        call cross-parity
          make across
          make down
      make no
  calm hold

# PARALLEL LOGICAL LOOPS COMMUTE: two winding strings on the SAME handle can be slid apart, crossing an even number of
# times, so they commute. crossing-commutes (cross-parity across across) = yes. The logical operators only fail to
# commute across the two DIFFERENT handles, which is exactly the qubit's X-Z pair.
rule parallel-loops-commute
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call crossing-commutes
        call cross-parity
          make across
          make across
      make yes
  calm hold

# THE TORUS GIVES TWO INDEPENDENT QUBITS: the crossing parity is anticommuting on the across-down pair AND on the
# down-across pair, the two handles in either order, while commuting on each handle with itself. The two off-diagonal
# anticommuting pairs are the two logical qubits, the fourfold ground-state degeneracy. Proved over all handle pairs by
# induction: crossing parity is symmetric.
rule cross-parity-is-symmetric
  seat a, like handle
  seat b, like handle
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call cross-parity
        read a
        read b
      call cross-parity
        read b
        read a
  fold a, b

# BRAIDING PHASE IS MINUS ONE: dragging an electric charge e all the way around a magnetic flux m crosses the flux's
# string an ODD number of times (once, the loop links the flux), so the wavefunction picks up a sign of minus one. We
# model the braiding sign as the crossing parity of the e-loop with the m-string: the across-down crossing is odd, the
# anticommuting flag no, the minus-one phase. This mutual statistics is the topological heart of the toric code, the
# signature of abelian anyons. Proved by computing the odd linking parity.
rule braiding-phase-is-minus-one
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call crossing-commutes
        call cross-parity
          make across
          make down
      make no
  calm hold

# TRIVIAL BRAIDING IS PLUS ONE: dragging an e around a region with NO flux, or along a contractible loop that links
# nothing, crosses zero strings, an even parity, and the phase is plus one. crossing-commutes (cross-parity across
# across) read as the braiding of two like-charges or a charge around empty space = yes, the plus-one phase. Only the
# e-around-m linking gives the nontrivial minus one.
rule trivial-braiding-is-plus-one
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call crossing-commutes
        call cross-parity
          make across
          make across
      make yes
  calm hold
